KERNEL MODE VS USER MODE 

USER MODE 👤

Normal applications run in User Mode.

Has limited privileges.

Cannot directly access critical hardware/resources.

Examples: Chrome, VS Code, C programs.

KERNEL MODE⚙️

OS Kernel runs in Kernel Mode.

Has high/full privileges.

// Privileges means special rights or permissions given to someone to do something.

Can access and control hardware, memory, CPU, files, etc.

Why are two modes needed?

To provide protection and security and prevent applications from damaging the OS or other processes.

How do they work?

Application
     ↓
User Mode
     ↓
System Call
     ↓
Kernel Mode
     ↓
OS performs operation
     ↓
Back to User Mode

Example 1

Program wants to read a file → asks OS through a system call → kernel accesses the disk → returns the data.
